CHAPTER 2: BACKGROUND AND SURVEY.
This chapter provides an in-depth overview of current studies, approaches, and technology involved in vendor performance evaluation, data analytics, and visualization systems. The aim of this review is to know existing methods, limitations and reasons to justify the necessity of the proposed Vendor Performance Analysis System.
The literature review is an introduction about the subject of the study.
The literature review is an inseparable part of any research project since it represents a pillar to the cognizance of already existing work in the field. It is useful in determining the gaps in existing methodologies, as well as providing information on how researchers and practitioners have dealt with similar issues.
When it comes to vendor performance analysis, different researchers have examined the methods of statistical assessment, multi-criteria evaluation decision-making, and data-driven analysis. As technology keeps growing and improving, new methods are more dependent on data analytics and other visualization tools to boost decision-making processes.
2.2 CONVENTIONAL VENDOR TESTING PROCESSES.
Conventional vendor evaluation approaches mainly rely on the manual evaluation strategies such as the use of scorecard, checklist and subjective ratings by the procurement managers. These techniques usually rank the vendors by considering factors like the price, quality, and delivery services.
Although these methods are direct and can be easily followed, they have various shortcomings. Manual evaluation is time consuming and subject to human bias and this may result in inconsistent results. Moreover, these approaches are not scaled and can not be used when there is a great amount of data.
Scientists have pointed out that conventional approaches lack real-time information and cannot support active business activities. Subsequently, there is an increasing desire to have automated and data-based systems substituting these approaches.
2.3 MULTI-CRITERIA DECISION-MAKING (MCDM) TECHNIQUES 
Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) methods have extensively been applied in the evaluation of vendors to cope with the complexity of a decision made under a number of factors. They are commonly used methods like the Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP), Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS) and Weighted Scoring Models.
One of the most common methods of selecting a vendor is AHP which has been proposed by Saaty. It entails organizing the decision criteria into hierarchy and a weighting of the criteria according to its significance. TOPSIS however works on placing vendors on a ranking of how far they are to an ideal solution.
In spite of the fact that MCDM methods offer a systematic way of determining a decision, they are still highly dependent on human factors to put weights and priorities into consideration. This adds subjectivity and constrains their efficacy in completely automated systems.
2.4 DATA ANALYTICS IN VENDOR PERFORMANCE EVALUATION.
As big data and sophisticated analytics start to emerge, organizations are turning to increasingly more data-driven methods of vendor analysis. The data analytics allow running a large amount of data to extract patterns, trends, and correlations that are difficult to see using a manual analysis.
A number of studies have indicated how it is effectively done using the statistical and analytical methods to assess the performance of the vendor. It is possible to examine metrics like delivery time, defect rate and cost variance that can give objective information on the efficiency of a vendor.
Vendor performance can also be continuously monitored with the help of data analytics that will help organizations to identify a problem at the initial stage and implement relevant measures. This reactive to proactive decision-making calculation is a great improvement to the conventional ways.
2.5 ATTRIBUTION VALUE OF DATA VISUALIZATION TOWARDS DECISION MAKING.
In order to visualize complex analytical outcomes in readily understandable formats, data visualization is highly important. Charts, graphs and dashboards are other who visualize data to allow users to learn trends and patterns.
Studies have established that visualization greatly enhances decision-making by declogging the head and optimising the understanding of data. The use of interactive dashboards, especially, gives users a chance to have an interactive experience and discover more information.
Visualization assists stakeholders in the vendor performance analysis to compare the vendors to each other and whether they are performing well or poorly and how the performance is growing. This renders it a very crucial aspect of the contemporary analytics systems.
2.6 ANALYTICAL APPLICATIONS OF DATABASE MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS.
Any analytical system must be efficient in data storage and management. Structured data are often stored in Relational Database Management Systems (RDBMS) like SQLite, MySQL and PostgreSQL.
Especially popular is SQLite, which can be used in applications of small to medium magnitude because of its simplicity and lightweight nature. It offers adequate features to handle structured data datasets without having to set up the server complexities.
Researchers lay emphasis on the fact that a properly-constructed database schema is key to ensuring data integrity, consensus and efficient querying. Scalability and performance are further improved by proper indexing and normalization techniques.
2.7 PYTHON-BASED ANALYTICAL SYSTEMS
The number of libraries available in Python managed to make it one of the most used programming languages for data analytics, including Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, etc. These libraries present a robust data manipulation, statistical analysis, and visualization tools.
Researchers and practitioners choose Python due to its simplicity, flexibility and the wide range of community support. It can be developed and integrated with databases and visualization structures fast and conveniently.
When applied to analyzing the performance of vendors, substantial datasets can be run through efficient analytical models applied in Python. It can also be used to perform exploratory data analysis and experimentation as it is compatible with Jupyter Notebooks.
2.8 DASHBOARD SYSTEMS BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE 
Dashboard systems are common in business intelligence application to show key performance indicators (KPIs) graphically. Exploratory tools in the form of Tableau, Power BI and custom-built dashboards offer interactive data interfaces.
Dashboards facilitate up to date tracking of business measurements and facilitate data-driven decision-making. They enable users to filter, drill down and conceive data in various aspects.
Dashboards are used in vendor performance analysis to give a centralized perspective of the vendor measures, where stakeholders find it easier to assess the performance and trends emerging. This improves responsibility and responsibility in vendor management processes.
2.9 DISADVANTAGES OF EXISTING SYSTEMS.
Although multiple methods and tools are available, the currently existing vendor evaluation systems have a number of shortcomings. The traditional techniques are not automatable and scalable whereas MCDM techniques depend on subjective inputs to a great extent.
Most data analytics solutions center on particular areas of vendor performance and are not a comprehensive solution. Also, not all systems have straightforward infrastructure and cannot be used in the small and medium-scale usage.
Many existing solutions also lack the integration of the elements of data storage, analysis and visualization. This disintegration compromises efficiency and makes the process of getting the overall picture of vendor performance hard.
2.10 RESEARCH GAP
The literature review notes that there is a gap loophole in the development of combined systems that enhance data manipulation, data storage and visualization to assess vendor activities. The majority of the current methods partake of the individual components but offer no comprehensive solution.
The system that will be able to automate the whole process of evaluating the vendors, including data collection and insight production, is required. It must be scalable, straightforward to use, as well as able to process real-life datasets.
The offered Vendor Performance Analysis System is going to bridge this gap and combine database management and data analytics with visualization in one place.
