4.2 System Architecture
It assumes a systematic architecture, which combines the data storage, processing, and visualization. It is made in a manner that would guarantee easy interaction between the various components and be in a modular way.
 
 
 
The architecture consists of three main layers:
1. Presentation Layer
This layer is the user interface layer that contains dashboards and visualizations. It enables users to engage the system, monitor performance indicators, and create reports. The layout is user-friendly and understandable so that those who will use it do not have to struggle to get the data.
2. Application Layer
Data processing and business logic are contained in the application layer. It consists of analytical Python scripts that read raw data and calculate performance measures. This layer is an intermediate between the database and the user interface.
3. Data Layer
Data layer is in charge of storing and management of data. It involves a SQLite database (inventory.db) in which data about vendors and transaction records can be stored. This layer guarantees integrity of data and effective retrieval.
4.3 Data Flow Diagram (DFD).
 

 

 
The Data Flow Diagram shows the flow of data within the system. It demonstrates the interplay between various elements and the conversion of input information into some useful output.
•	Level 0 DFD (Context Diagram)
The Level 0 DFD shows the system as one entity that communicates with external users. The input data are presented by the users, and the system processes it to give outputs in the form of reports and visualizations.
•	Level 1 DFD
The L1 DFD is subdivided into smaller operations, i.e., data input, processing, storage and visualization. It depicts the flow of data amongst these parts and the role of individual module towards the overall functionality.
The DFD assists in comprehending the logical path of data and makes certain that all the processes are interlinked.
4.4 Database Design
The design of the database is an important part of the system that defines the mechanism of data storage, organization and retrieval. An effective database offers consistency of data and effective querying.
 
 
The database will have several tables such as:
•	Vendor Table
This table records data on the vendors (vendors ID, name, contact, and category). It is the leading organization within the system.
•	Transaction Table
This table records transactions between the organization and vendors. It contains information like date of order, delivery date, quantity and cost.
•	Performance Metrics Table
The metrics calculated in the table are delivery delays, defect rates, and cost efficiency. These are metrics applied in measuring the performance of the vendors.
Primary and foreign keys are used to establish the relationships between tables thereby guaranteeing the data integrity and consistency.

4.5 Module Design
The system is broken down according to individual modules that perform a certain task. This type of modular style enhances the maintenance ease and enables easy updates and additions.
•	Data Collection Module
This is the module that will be in charge of gathering and storing the vendors data into the database. It makes sure that data is typed correctly and uniformly.
•	Data Processing Module
This module works with raw data and calculates the metrics of performance. It covers data cleaning, transformation as well as data analysis.
•	Visualization Module
This module creates charts and dashboards in which the performance measures will be shown. It is concerned with the way of displaying information in an understandable way.
•	Reporting Module
This module produces reports on the basis of analyzed data. Reports can consist of summaries, comparison and performance evaluation.
4.6 3-Tier Architecture
The system is three-tier based architecture that divides the application into three different layers. Scalability, security, and maintainability are enhanced.
 

‘
